This thesis addresses the challenge of conducting molecular property prediction when the underlying experimental data contain noisy labels. Most existing work on noise in QSAR modeling assumes that label noise can be modeled as random error drawn from a Gaussian distribution, and evaluates either molecular representations or predictive model architectures in isolation. \citet{Cortes2015} examined 12 algorithms across multiple noise levels but used fixed representations. Studies that compare representations are typically done on clean data. No work has systematically compared the relative importance of representation versus architecture choice for noise robustness, assessed whether probabilistic methods justify their computational overhead in noisy settings, or tested whether findings generalize across different corruption patterns and molecular property types. The first half of this thesis therefore asks four questions about the behavior of QSAR models under label noise.\label{sec:intro:questions} First, how does the choice of molecular representation and model architecture explain the variance in predictive performance, and does that division change when we instead consider how much performance a QSAR model retains under increasing label noise? Second, what does the choice of model provide under label noise? Within this question we compare deterministic and probabilistic modeling. Third, does the type of label noise matter, or just the quantity of it? We generate and add label noise in several ways, both to clean data and to data with inherent experimental noise. Fourth, what is the impact of artificial label noise on a model's uncertainty estimates? Does the estimated uncertainty rise with increased label noise, and can it point to which labels are corrupted? Together, these questions aim to identify what, if anything, makes a QSAR model robust to noise. To answer them we introduce NoiseInject, an open-source Python package for benchmarking noise robustness and uncertainty quantification in machine learning models.

