We hypothesized that the distance itself was at fault. On a given dataset, most features of a general-purpose representation either do not vary or do not relate to the property. Every such feature adds a term to the distance, so together these irrelevant features dominate it. Their contribution grows linearly with the number of features, not exponentially. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} measures this on base representations and on the distances computed from them. The fix we propose is to select a fixed number of task-relevant features from several representations, and to compute distances in that compact space. A first version showed that these selected feature sets also work as ordinary model inputs, with no distance involved. The question then became how to choose the features, which is the subject of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} returns to distances, noise detection and LNL, including noise mitigation, with the hybrid representation as the feature space.

