This chapter introduces the ideas that every later chapter refers back to. We first describe molecular representations, which turn a molecule into a fixed-length vector of features, and the model families that learn from them. We then set out the assumption that similar molecules have similar properties, and where it breaks down. Next we describe where label noise in real assay data comes from, the seven artificial noise conditions used in this thesis, and the methods for learning with noisy labels. The chapter ends with NoiseInject, the package that injects the noise and reports robustness and uncertainty metrics, and with the group-aware splits and metrics used throughout.




\section{Molecular Representations in QSAR}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

% CODE INPUT: first person changed to "we"
The success of machine learning hinges on the quality of input data. Although the quality can refer to noise, which most of this work centers on, here we mean the type of input labels. Molecular representations provide different features with which QSAR models and noise detection techniques can be learned. However, a potential bottleneck on larger data sets should be noted; the more complex the data structure, the greater the computational cost. For smaller data sets, the number of parameters is limited by the size of the training set, to prevent overfitting. 

