Support vector regression fits a function that is as flat as possible while keeping training errors small \citep{Vapnik1995, Smola2004}. It uses the $\epsilon$-insensitive loss: errors smaller than $\epsilon$ cost nothing, and larger errors cost their size minus $\epsilon$. A constant $C$ trades flatness against tolerance of large errors, and classification uses the same machinery with a maximum-margin boundary \citep{Cortes1995}. The features enter only through the kernel. With the RBF kernel this is a function of Euclidean distance, so every feature enters every distance, and we standardize features before fitting. Three properties suggest robustness to label noise: errors inside the $\epsilon$ tube change nothing, the loss outside it grows linearly rather than quadratically, and each molecule's weight is capped at $C$ \citep{Smola2004}. Under simulated experimental error, the RBF-kernel SVM degraded smoothly and linearly \citep{Cortes2015}; Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} reports how it holds up under each of our noise conditions. The main limitation is cost: training works on a kernel matrix that grows with the square of the number of training molecules \citep{Chang2011}.


