Much of cheminformatics rests on the similar property principle: molecules that are structurally similar tend to have similar properties \citep{Johnson1990, vanTilborg2022}. 
One of the clearest applications of this principle is the use of nearest-neighbor prediction which shows up across cheminformatics workflows and in the case of this research, in the kernels used by GPs and at the basis of many noise identification algorithms.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
The difficulty with this assumption is that similarity is defined only relative to a similarity measure, and that measure is fixed by the choice of representation and
coefficient \citep{Maggiora2014}. There is no standard definition of similarity between molecules, especially when handling different molecular embeddings in a computational setting. Two molecules that are near-identical under a substructure fingerprint may be far apart under a physicochemical descriptor vector. \citet{Maggiora2014} caution that quantified similarity can be over-interpreted in this manner.

% CODE INPUT: formula added so that Chapter 4's argument about distances has its starting point here
The most common measure for binary fingerprints is the Tanimoto coefficient \citep{Bajusz2015}. For two molecules with fingerprints $\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}' \in \{0,1\}^d$,
\begin{equation}
T(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{d} x_j x'_j}{\sum_{j=1}^{d} x_j + \sum_{j=1}^{d} x'_j - \sum_{j=1}^{d} x_j x'_j},
\label{eq:tanimoto}
\end{equation}
the number of bits the two molecules share, divided by the number of bits set in either. Continuous representations are compared with a distance instead, most often the Euclidean distance $\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{d}(x_j - x'_j)^2}$. Both are sums over the features, and neither weighs a feature by whether it relates to the property. In the Tanimoto coefficient, a bit that is off in both molecules adds nothing, but every bit that is set in either molecule counts. In the Euclidean distance, every feature that differs between the two molecules counts. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} returns to this (Equation~\ref{eq:distance_split}). 

There are many downstream tasks which rely on the concept of similarity. The second half of this thesis relies on many such tasks, and as such focuses on identifying representations that agree with this principle. 


