Learning with noisy labels (LNL) covers every method for training a model when some of its labels are wrong. We divide it into two parts. Noise mitigation changes, drops or reweights the training labels once, before any model is trained, and any model can then be fitted to the result. Its methods include removal, down-weighting, smoothing, relabeling and correction. Noise-robust training leaves the labels alone and changes how the model trains. Its methods include robust losses, noise-modeling layers, regularization, and reweighting inside the training loop, as in co-teaching and Meta-Weight-Net. A sample-selection step taken out of its training loop, and used only to delete molecules before a refit, counts as noise mitigation. Noise detection and noise estimation find noisy labels or estimate how many there are. They feed noise mitigation, but are not LNL methods themselves. \citet{Song2022} sort 62 deep-learning methods into five groups: robust architecture, robust regularization, robust loss function, loss adjustment and sample selection.
% CODE INPUT
No single method is expected to win. Averaged over all problems, no learning algorithm beats another \citep{Wolpert1996, Wolpert1997}. The chemistry analog is a study of twelve algorithms on twelve bioactivity datasets with simulated experimental errors \citep{Cortes2015}. Neighbor-based cleaning goes back to editing a training set by its nearest neighbors \citep{Wilson1972}. It relies on similar molecules having similar labels, and activity cliffs break that assumption. QSAR models also frequently fail to predict activity cliffs \citep{Dablander2023}. Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} tests this.


