Two different questions can be asked of a distance. The first is about all pairs: does distance track the difference in property across the whole dataset? Plotting descriptor distance against activity difference for every pair is the neighborhood-behavior plot of \citet{Patterson1996}. We summarize that plot with one number, the Spearman correlation between pair distance and absolute label difference. The second question is about nearest neighbors only: can each molecule's label be predicted from its five nearest neighbors? The prediction is the mean label of the five. The neighbors can carry signal even when the all-pairs correlation is near zero. In what follows, each claim says which of the two it is about.

We computed both for every representation in the pool of each of the seven regression datasets. Six are the small-molecule and QM9 datasets of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}. The seventh is HLM clearance, which is not in that table. Representations with only 0/1 features were compared by Tanimoto distance, $1-T$. All others were compared by Manhattan distance on standardized features, leaving out features that are constant across the dataset. Manhattan distance is defined in the next subsection. For the all-pairs correlation, each dataset over 2,000 molecules was cut to a fixed random sample of 2,000 molecules (seed 0). That gives 1,999,000 pairs per dataset. hERG $K_i$ used all 996,166 pairs of its 1,412 molecules. For the neighbor prediction, all molecules were used, and each molecule's five neighbors came from other scaffold groups. No molecule is therefore predicted from its own scaffold.

