A similarity can also be learned. \citet{Coupry2022} learn a molecular similarity from graph embeddings, but separately from any endpoint. It is still not chosen for the property being predicted. Earlier work did choose the similarity for the task. \citet{Zheng2000} predict each molecule's activity as the average of its $k$ most similar molecules, using Euclidean distance over descriptors. They select the subset of descriptors by simulated annealing to maximize the leave-one-out $q^2$. \citet{Itskowitz2005} extend this to optimize the descriptor subset, the number of neighbors and the neighbor weighting function together. Both select features for a nearest-neighbor model of one endpoint. The method in this thesis selects features across several representations. Every method in Section~\ref{sec:flaws:consequences} takes a similarity as input and inherits the dilution described here. ``Similarity in a hybrid'' (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}) tests whether a feature set chosen for the task gives a better similarity.

