Here, we present the adaptive hybrid representation, or ``hybrid'' for short. It takes a collection of feature matrices for a given dataset and outputs a customized feature set optimized for a specific prediction task. The inputs can be any type of frozen feature vector, such as fingerprints, descriptor sets, foundation model embeddings, gene expression signatures, histopathology features. Within a given domain, these individual vectors typically contain their own type of information. It's a well-established phenomenon when it comes to machine learning, there's rarely ever a one-size-fits-all approach. Take the noise robustness study in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} as evidence, with NGBoost on clean labels. On QM9, NGBoost's held-out $R^2$ was highest with PDV input features (0.865, median of 10 replicates). MHG-GNN was only 0.013 lower (0.852), and ECFP4 was lowest (0.706). On LogD, PDV was again highest (0.673), with MHG-GNN second (0.658). On hERG $K_i$, MHG-GNN was highest (0.511). PDV was second (0.456), the median of the 4 of 5 folds whose clean $R^2$ was at least 0.3. On Caco-2, NGBoost appeared to prefer Avalon (0.402), with ChemBERTa at 0.404. Both numbers are medians of only 2 of 5 folds; the other 3 fell below a clean $R^2$ of 0.3 and were excluded. Over all 5 folds, PDV is highest (0.370), ahead of Avalon (0.287) and ChemBERTa (0.256). The model NGBoost itself produces better fits when given physicochemical properties, yet this preference can be overridden by a different prediction task. Accuracy and robustness also pick different representations. On QM9 under Gaussian noise, NGBoost's highest noise robustness (\aucnorm, the normalized area under the $R^2$ against noise level curve) was on Avalon, 0.982 against 0.975 for PDV.

But as important as the selection of useful features is, it is also important to filter out the useless features. We could throw the kitchen sink at the models and pool together features across a wide array of representations. Not only would that pose computational challenges, but it also introduces a huge set of irrelevant features that function as noise in downstream applications (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}). With all of that in mind, I decided to develop a method that selects the best features for a given data set and model. In theory, it should effectively filter out noisy features and isolate the feature which carry the most relevant information.

