We score features using LightGBM gain importance, SHAP values for tree ensembles (TreeSHAP), and DeepLIFT (Deep Learning Important FeaTures) \citep{Shrikumar2017}. They come from three families: split gain, Shapley values and reference-based attribution. LightGBM gain measures how much a feature reduced training loss where trees split on it \citep{ke2017lightgbm}. Its model is 50 gradient-boosted trees of maximum depth 6. A feature's score is the loss reduction of every split on it, summed over all trees, and 0 if no tree splits on it. TreeSHAP measures how much a feature moves each molecule's prediction, as an exact Shapley value for tree models \citep{Lundberg2020}. Its model is a random forest of 50 trees with maximum depth 10 \citep{Breiman2001}. A feature's score is its mean absolute Shapley value over the training molecules. DeepLIFT measures how much a feature moves a small network's output away from its output on a reference input \citep{Shrikumar2017, Kokhlikyan2020}. Its model is a small neural network with two hidden layers, of 64 and 32 ReLU units. It is trained for 100 full-batch epochs of Adam at a learning rate of 0.001 \citep{Kingma2015}. A feature's score is its mean absolute attribution over the training molecules, under DeepLIFT's Rescale rule. The reference input is all zeros, which after standardizing is every feature at its training mean. For a fingerprint bit, that is the fraction of training molecules that have the bit. On binary labels, TreeSHAP and DeepLIFT use the positive class. On multiclass labels, they average absolute values over the classes. For LightGBM gain and TreeSHAP, a representation wider than 2,000 features is pre-screened. Only its 2,000 highest-variance features, with variance measured before standardizing, are fitted; the rest score 0. DeepLIFT scores every feature. On Caco-2, the median share of features scoring above 0 was 30\% for LightGBM gain, 70\% for TreeSHAP and 90\% for DeepLIFT. That median is over 10 representations and 3 folds, and is preliminary. Table~\ref{tab:importance_methods} summarizes the three methods.

