The global pool sizes each representation's offer with squared weights $\tilde r_k^{\,2}$, as shares of a pool of $2B$ candidate features. An offer is capped at the representation's width in features. Only features scoring above 0 are offered. The offered features are joined into one matrix. The same importance method is then fitted once on that matrix, on the internal training part. The hybrid keeps the top $B$ features of this joint ranking, again only those scoring above 0. Each representation's final count is an outcome of the joint ranking, not set in advance, and there is no one-feature floor. A representation gets 0 features when its score is 0 or when none of its candidates reach the joint top $B$. Here a feature's rank depends on which features the other representations offered. Under fixed shares it does not. The cost is one extra importance fit per configuration, on the pooled candidates.

