For a fair comparison, the downstream models must cover a range of inductive biases. They run from methods that degrade in high dimensions (SVM, GP) to tree ensembles that tolerate irrelevant features. Standard experiments use five downstream models: random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, SVM and DNN, with a Gaussian process added on humanness. An experiment that uses a model outside these five names it in its own methods. We skip the two models whose fitting cost grows fastest with the number of molecules on large datasets: Gaussian processes above 1,000 samples, SVMs above 10,000. No per-dataset tuning is performed: all models use fixed hyperparameters throughout, so performance differences reflect the input, not the model configuration.

% CODE INPUT
The five downstream models cover bagged trees (random forest), boosted trees (XGBoost and LightGBM), a kernel method (SVM) and a neural network (DNN). We use five because the best set of features depends on the model that uses it. A hybrid that helped only one model family would be a property of that model, not a better input. Tree ensembles are the baseline to beat. A hybrid is a tabular feature matrix, and tree-based models still lead on medium-sized tabular data of about 10,000 samples \citep{Grinsztajn2022}. A Gaussian process \citep{Rasmussen2006} is added as a sixth model on datasets under 1,000 molecules. Three of the 16 datasets are that small: humanness (564 sequences), nanobody thermostability (672 sequences) and breast cancer PAM50 subtype (821 tumors). The Gaussian process runs on humanness only. Humanness is a classification task, so the Gaussian process there has an RBF kernel. The regression version, with an RBF kernel plus a white-noise term, runs on none of the 16 datasets. PAM50 subtype is left out under every augmentation setting for run time. In one search, the one with augmentation features added on top of the budget, the Gaussian process took 44 of its 60 hours. Nanobody thermostability is left out under augmentation on top of the budget, where its searches did not finish in the time limit. A dataset that drops the Gaussian process under any setting gets it under none, so nanobody thermostability is left out everywhere.

% CODE INPUT
Every model uses the same settings on every dataset, so differences between scores come from the input. There is no per-dataset tuning by default. An optional per-fold random search exists. It tries 20 draws of a model's settings, scored in the dataset's own metric on 3 group-aware inner folds of the outer training fold only. The DNN is never tuned this way; it relies on early stopping. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: whether tuning pays on base representations, tuned against untuned models, in each dataset's metric]}. Two models are skipped above a cap on the number of molecules. The Gaussian process is skipped above 1,000 molecules, because it builds and factorizes the full molecule-by-molecule kernel. The SVM is skipped above 10,000 molecules. Above that, its kernel cache no longer holds the support vectors, and a fit stops finishing in useful time. Under augmentation on top of the budget, the SVM is skipped above 5,000 molecules. The caps count the molecules the search is given. The search run once per dataset is given the whole dataset. A search inside one outer fold is given that fold's training molecules, about 80\% of the dataset. On two datasets the two counts give different downstream models, both under augmentation on top of the budget. LogD has 5,028 molecules and TATA promoter 5,274. The SVM is dropped when the whole dataset is counted, and kept when about 4,000 training molecules are counted. Some default models are also not run on named datasets where a single fit was too slow. They are listed in Additional file~\ref{app:add:models_left_out}. Every downstream model's settings, library, molecule cap and citation are in Table~\ref{tab:add:model_settings} (Additional files).

