\subsubsection{Label smoothing and sample weighting}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION -- the two bulleted lists (label smoothing, sample weighting) are now Table tab:noise_corrections. Checked against src/kirby/utils/noise_estimation.py on 2026-09-29/30: scores are min-max normalized to [0, 1] before use; only the soft weight is clipped to [0.1, 1], and all weights are then rescaled to mean 1, so a final weight can exceed 1.
Given the fact that all labels contain some magnitude of noise, we focused strongly on label smoothing over removal. Rather than only modifying labels, we also weighted more trustworthy samples during training. Both use the noise score after min-max normalization to $[0, 1]$, written $\tilde s_i$ (Table~\ref{tab:noise_corrections}).

One row is one way of using the noise score. $\alpha$ sets the strength of each rule. The target is the mean label of the molecule's nearest neighbors; $\mu$ and $\sigma$ are a probabilistic model's predicted mean and standard deviation.
\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\caption[Label smoothing and sample weighting rules.]{Label smoothing and sample weighting rules. Weights are rescaled to mean 1 after they are computed, to preserve the effective sample size.}
\label{tab:noise_corrections}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.17\linewidth}p{0.13\linewidth}p{0.62\linewidth}@{}}
\toprule
Rule & Changes & New label or weight of molecule $i$ \\
\midrule
Proportional & Label & $y_i + \alpha\,\tilde s_i\,(\text{target} - y_i)$ \\
Threshold & Label & Only above the $100(1-\alpha)$th percentile of $\tilde s$: $(1-\alpha)\,y_i + \alpha\cdot\text{target}$, so $\alpha$ sets both how many labels move and how far \\
Soft threshold & Label & Correction strength $\alpha \cdot \operatorname{sigmoid}\!\big(5(\tilde s_i - 0.5)\big)$ \\
Confidence & Label & $(1-c_i)\,y_i + c_i\,\mu_i$, with $c_i = \alpha\sigma_i / (1 + \alpha\sigma_i)$; for estimators that output $\sigma$, such as NGBoost \\
Inverse & Weight & $1 / (1 + \alpha\,\tilde s_i)$ \\
Exponential & Weight & $\exp(-\alpha\,\tilde s_i)$ \\
Soft & Weight & $1 - \alpha\,\tilde s_i^{\,2}$, clipped to $[0.1, 1]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

