\subsubsection{Recovery rate}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

For noise detection accuracy, we compute precision, recall, and F1 score, treating noise injection as ground truth.

% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION -- formula checked against the code on 2026-09-29: the experiments in this chapter compute recovery per downstream model, in the dataset's own metric, as a percentage, and leave it undefined when the clean-to-noisy gap is 2% of the clean score or less (tests/experiments/noise/n11_estimation_remediation_grid.py:300, 1457, 1511; same in n07, n32). The R^2 / random forest on ECFP4 version describes the exploratory study only (Table tab:code_input_neighbor_recovery). Your original paragraph is kept commented out below.
Each downstream model $m$ is trained and tested three times: on the clean training labels, giving the score $s^{m}_{\text{clean}}$; on the noisy labels, giving $s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}$; and on the labels after noise estimation and correction, giving $s^{m}_{\text{denoised}}$. Scores are in the dataset's own metric, averaged over folds. The recovery rate is the share of the loss that correction wins back,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Recovery}_m = 100 \times \frac{s^{m}_{\text{denoised}} - s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}}{s^{m}_{\text{clean}} - s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}}\ \%,
\label{eq:recovery}
\end{equation}
which reads the same way whether the metric is higher- or lower-is-better. Each model is divided by its own clean-to-noisy gap. When that gap is no more than $2\%$ of the clean score, the noise did too little damage to recover, and the recovery rate is left undefined rather than reported. A recovery rate of $100\%$ indicates complete restoration to clean performance; negative values indicate that correction made performance worse than the noisy labels. The exploratory study reused in Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} computed the same ratio in $R^2$, with one random forest on ECFP4 as the only downstream model.
