Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} asked four questions about label noise: how the representation, the model and the type of noise shape a QSAR model, and whether its uncertainty finds the noisy labels. For accuracy, model and representation explained comparable shares of the variance in R$^2$. On the QM9 HOMO--LUMO gap, with Gaussian noise as wide as the clean training labels, the model explained 29.1\% and the representation 26.6\%. For robustness, measured as \aucnorm, the model dominated. Under Gaussian noise it explained 49.5\% of the variance, against 9.2\% for the representation. Changing the shape of the noise barely changed performance; grouped-shifted and censored noise cost the most. Median \aucnorm{} was 0.813 under censoring and 0.951 under Gaussian noise, over the same five pairings of model and representation. Censoring was run on those five pairings only. Mean predicted uncertainty rose with the amount of noise, but no model's per-molecule uncertainty found the corrupted labels. Under six of the seven noise conditions, the Spearman correlation between a molecule's predicted uncertainty and its injected noise stayed between $-0.16$ and $0.16$. Under censoring, the models were most confident on the clipped labels.

