This thesis set out to predict molecular properties from labels that carry experimental noise. The first half asked what makes a QSAR model robust to that noise. Model architecture matters more than representation for how much performance a model keeps as noise rises. The shape of the noise distribution matters little. Noise shared within a scaffold family, and labels clipped at an assay limit, do the most damage. Predicted uncertainty rises with the amount of noise, but it does not pick out which labels are corrupted.

