\section{Small molecules}\label{app:datasets:smallmol}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

% TODO: review, needs manual review
Three steps are shared by several small-molecule datasets. The first is structure standardization with RDKit \citep{rdkit}. The steps run in this order: cleanup, keep the largest fragment (removing salts and solvents), neutralize charges, and choose the canonical tautomer. Any molecule that then contains a metal atom is rejected, and the result is written as canonical SMILES. Standardization is applied to hERG $K_i$, LogD and Caco-2. The Biogen sets, QM9 and the hERG blocker sets are only checked to parse in RDKit.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
The second shared step removes duplicate molecules by InChIKey \citep{Heller2015}, keeping the first occurrence. Two different SMILES strings can describe the same molecule, and without this step one molecule could sit in both a training fold and a test fold. The step is applied to hERG $K_i$, LogD, Caco-2, the two Biogen sets and QM9. The hERG blocker sets are deduplicated by InChIKey only against the other sites, as described below. The step is skipped on purpose for hERG IC$_{50}$, which keeps one row per measurement. The third shared step is the fold grouping by Bemis--Murcko scaffold \citep{Bemis1996}, whose form in the hybrid experiments is given in the Datasets and splitting subsection of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{\texorpdfstring{hERG $K_i$.}{hERG Ki.}} Records for the hERG potassium channel (ChEMBL target CHEMBL240) were retrieved from ChEMBL \citep{Zdrazil2023} through its web interface. The query keeps records with standard type $K_i$, a pChEMBL value present, an exact relation (``$=$''), and no curator data-validity comment. Records reported as ``$<$'' or ``$>$'' a value are therefore never included. Only binding assays (ChEMBL assay type B) are kept. The label is the pChEMBL value, $\mathrm{p}K_i = -\log_{10} K_i$ with $K_i$ in molar units. Repeat measurements of one compound are merged into their median, and compounds whose repeats have a standard deviation above 1.0 p$K_i$ unit are removed, following \citet{landrum2024}. The structures are then standardized and deduplicated by InChIKey. Because repeats are merged on ChEMBL's own SMILES before standardization, two ChEMBL entries that standardize to the same molecule are not merged by median; the InChIKey step keeps the first of them. On a pull of September 2026, 1,815 $K_i$ records reduce to 1,812 with a pChEMBL value and 1,737 from binding assays. These give 1,415 compounds, 226 of them measured more than once and none removed by the standard-deviation rule. The InChIKey step removes 3 more, leaving 1,412 compounds with p$K_i$ from 4.07 to 9.41. The file read by the experiments was pulled on 1 February 2026; the ChEMBL release was not recorded at the time. Re-running the same query in September 2026 against ChEMBL release 37 gives a byte-identical file of 1,415 compounds.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{LogD and Caco-2 efflux.} Both endpoints come from the training split of the OpenADMET--ExpansionRx challenge data, released on Hugging Face under a CC-BY-4.0 license \citep{openadmet}. The file holds 5,326 molecules; 5,039 have a LogD value and 2,161 a Caco-2 efflux ratio. For each endpoint, rows without a value are dropped and the structures are standardized. Rows that standardize to the same SMILES are merged into their median label. This merges 11 LogD pairs and 3 Caco-2 pairs. The efflux ratio, which has no unit, is then transformed to $\log_{10}$. Labels are clipped at the dataset's own 1st and 99th percentiles: $-1.0$ and $4.5$ for LogD, and $-0.319$ and $1.679$ for $\log_{10}$ efflux ratio. Clipping sets 116 LogD labels (54 low, 62 high) and 45 Caco-2 labels (23 low, 22 high) to a limit. The extreme labels of both sets are therefore flattened before any noise is added in Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}. The InChIKey step removes nothing on either set, leaving 5,028 and 2,158 molecules.
% TODO(lit): pH at which the OpenADMET LogD values were measured (not stated on the Hugging Face page).

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{Rat liver microsome clearance and MDR1--MDCK efflux.} These are two endpoints of the Biogen ADME set of 3,521 compounds \citep{fang2023}, downloaded from Polaris as \texttt{biogen/adme-fang-v1} \citep{polaris}. Rat liver microsome clearance is the intrinsic clearance $\mathrm{CL_{int}}$ in mL/min/kg, and the MDR1--MDCK endpoint is an efflux ratio. Both are released already as $\log_{10}$ values and are used as released. Rows without a value, or whose SMILES RDKit cannot parse, are dropped, and duplicates are removed by InChIKey. There is no standardization, no median merge and no clipping. Clearance has 3,054 values and efflux 2,642; the InChIKey step removes one molecule from each, leaving 3,053 and 2,641.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{QM9.} QM9 \citep{Ramakrishnan2014, qm9_dataset} was read through PyTorch Geometric \citep{pytorchGeometric}, which distributes 130,831 molecules. Of these, 129,428 parse in RDKit; the other 1,403 carry an over-valent carbon atom in the released structure file. This list of parsable molecules is identical to the one used in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}. From it, 10,000 molecules were drawn once at random with seed 42, so the subsample is a random draw of molecules, not a random split. The InChIKey step removes 22 duplicates, leaving 9,978. The label is the HOMO--LUMO gap in eV, from 1.88 to 11.37 eV in this sample.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{hERG blockers held at separate sites.} The separate-site experiments of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} use two further hERG sets from Lhasa Limited: a training set of 7,772 molecules (3,630 blockers, 46.7\%) and a test set of 4,383 molecules (1,084 blockers). Both were provided through the Effiris and FLuID collaboration \citep{Bassani2023, Hanser2023}. Each label is Lhasa's own binary blocker call, and the two sets were labeled under different rules. The molecules are stored as RDKit molecule objects and converted to SMILES. Entries that give no valid SMILES are dropped, which removes none. Neither set is standardized. The sets are proprietary and cannot be redistributed (Appendix~\ref{app:availability}). In the same experiments the hERG $K_i$ set acts as a third site. Its labels are made binary, with a compound counted as a blocker when $\mathrm{p}K_i \ge 6.0$ ($K_i \le 1\,\mu$M), which gives 467 blockers and 945 non-blockers. Molecules are then matched by InChIKey across the three sites, taken in the order hERG $K_i$, training set, test set, and only the first copy is kept. This removes 166 molecules from the training set and 170 from the test set, all of them also in the hERG $K_i$ set, leaving 7,606 and 4,213. The two Lhasa sets share no molecule. For 56 of the 166 and 57 of the 170 shared molecules, the Lhasa blocker call disagrees with the call from p$K_i$. The training and test sets fall into 4,002 and 2,298 scaffold groups before this step, and 3,903 and 2,222 after it.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{\texorpdfstring{hERG IC$_{50}$.}{hERG IC50.}} This set is used only in Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}, where the disagreement between repeat measurements and the curator flags serve as real evidence of label error. It keeps every measurement as its own row. The query takes ChEMBL records for CHEMBL240 with standard type IC$_{50}$ in nM and an exact relation \citep{Zdrazil2023}. Records with a missing or non-positive value are dropped, and the label is $\mathrm{pIC_{50}} = 9 - \log_{10}(\mathrm{IC_{50}}/\mathrm{nM})$. No assay-type filter is applied. Of the 12,010 records, 10,219 come from binding assays, 1,543 from toxicity assays, 217 from functional assays and 31 from ADME assays, as ChEMBL classifies them. Records with a curator data-validity comment are kept and marked; there are 301 of them, on 215 molecules. There is no standardization, no median merge and no InChIKey step. The result is 12,008 measurements of 9,802 molecules. Folds are grouped by scaffold, and every measurement of a molecule shares that molecule's scaffold, so no molecule is split between training and test.

